WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:12.510
PALI BHAT: Today, we want to
talk about Cloud DevOps, right?

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.440
So when we talk about
DevOps, most people

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.140
think this, which
is, it's a great way

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.570
to connect the development
and operations teams

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.810
and bridge the gap.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:26.170
Yes, this is true, but DevOps
is a lot more than just that.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.780
Ultimately, what
DevOps can do is

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.720
get your business more agility.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.210
It can bring feedback from
all of the downstream systems,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.100
including your customers, back
into the development process

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.490
so that you can build great
solutions for your customers.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:47.070
So as we think about
Cloud DevOps at Google,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.150
there's three principles
that we keep in mind.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.500
The first, of course,
is how do we increase

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.520
developer productivity, because
that's what we're all here for.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.640
The second bit, which
is just as important,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.040
is getting all of that
downstream feedback

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.130
and shifting it left so
developers have access to it

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.720
earlier in the cycle.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.710
What this lets you do is
avoid those costly mistakes

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.760
and catch them
much, much sooner.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.320
And the third piece,
which just as important,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.800
is scaled operations.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.560
What the cloud lets you do is
run your business at scale,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.788
and Cloud DevOps
should enable that.

00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:29.330
So we're going to talk a little
bit about each of these three

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.150
aspects, and then dive
into a fireside chat

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.630
as well in a little bit of time.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.900
So first, let's talk
about how we increase

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.190
developer productivity.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.150
As we talk to our customers,
the number one thing

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:48.680
we are hearing from
them is, yes, they've

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.040
looked at Cloud as a way
to lower their costs.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.440
But they want a lot
more than just that.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.440
They want greater agility
for their business.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.010
And the way they want to do
this is by building cloud

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.330
native applications.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.010
There's been a huge
surge of interest

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:08.360
in this, especially
around Kubernetes.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.840
Unfortunately,
though, active teams

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.290
across a number of our
customers are still

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:20.180
working on traditional IDEs
that are optimized for local app

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:20.810
development.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:21.755
Those run on premise.

00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:27.830
And one of the
challenges with this

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.610
is as you use these local
environments to build cloud

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.990
native applications,
you frequently end up

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:40.670
with either more complexity
or errors that are

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.980
caught too late in the cycle.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.930
So to solve this problem, we
announced Cloud Code yesterday.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.680
And what Cloud Code
lets you do is rapidly

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.190
build debug and, of course,
to apply these Kubernetes

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.960
applications.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.920
And the best part is you
can do all of that right

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.090
within the context of your
local development environment.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.870
Think of this as a
power boost to your ID.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:14.260
And we've shifted left
all of the complexity,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.190
and we've abstracted it away
so you can actually just focus

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.810
on building cloud native apps.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.330
To tell you more
about this, I want

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:26.560
to welcome on stage Jez Humble,
who's part of our developer

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:27.410
advocacy team.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:27.910
Jez?

00:03:35.065 --> 00:03:37.460
JEZ HUMBLE: So how many
people in this room

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:43.910
are building codes in nodes,
or Go, or Java, or Python?

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.050
OK, that's a lot of you.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.280
Great.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.340
How many people know how to
set up and manage a Kubernetes

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.940
cluster?

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.370
OK, that's also a lot of you.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.360
That's great.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.520
How many of you are
going to be working

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.330
on Kubernetes in the next year?

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.740
OK, almost everyone.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:02.900
So that's great.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.230
Kubernetes is the industry
standard infrastructure

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.552
for building cloud native apps.

00:04:08.552 --> 00:04:10.010
But if you're
anything like me, you

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:11.450
might find Kubernetes
a little bit

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.570
intimidating to
set up and manage.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.750
You might also worry about the
impacts on your productivity.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.019
My favorite experience
as a developer

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.530
is being able to save in the
IDE, switch to the browser,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.710
hit Refresh, and be able to
see pretty much instantly what

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.540
I did so that I can check
that I'm on the right track.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.760
Microservices and
service-oriented architectures

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.300
also add complexity, and we want
to create a simple way for you

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.360
to stand up some subset of
your production environment

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.670
for development
purposes locally.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.390
So today, we're going
to show you Cloud Code.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.740
Cloud Code lets you develop
codes in VS code and IntelliJ

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.410
and get a seamless,
near-instant developer

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.500
experience that
mimics what you're

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.750
used to, that local
developer workflow.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.930
So to show you that, we're
going to build and deploy

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.790
a simple distributed system.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.320
This is a guest
book which is going

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.620
to talk to a microservice
that does translation

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.200
of the comments in the guest
book into different languages.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.770
We're going to start off
by building and deploying

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.205
the translation microservices.

00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:18.580
And Dan [INAUDIBLE]
here is going

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.132
to show us what that looks
like in Java using IntelliJ.

00:05:22.132 --> 00:05:23.090
SPEAKER 1: Thanks, Jez.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.853
Thanks, everyone, for coming.

00:05:24.853 --> 00:05:27.270
Everyone here has heard of
terms like continuous delivery,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.540
continuous integration,
continuous deployment.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:30.380
Today, I'm going
to be showing how

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.880
to use Cloud Code for
IntelliJ to set up

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.900
a continuous development loop
where I can edit and save

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.858
my code locally, and it's
going to get deployed

00:05:35.858 --> 00:05:37.970
and running inside of a
cloud Kubernetes cluster

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.810
inside of Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.620
So right here, we have a cloud
native guest book application.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.370
It's a very simple
Hello World service.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.420
It was written in Java,
and it's printing out

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:48.952
hello world over HTTP.

00:05:48.952 --> 00:05:50.410
Now we can see that
this is running

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.950
in our continuous
development loop.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.170
So it's running here inside
of a remote Kubernetes

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:56.100
cluster in GKE.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.940
Hit refresh, see that
we have the message.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.104
I want to come in here
and change this message

00:06:03.104 --> 00:06:04.692
and press Save.

00:06:04.692 --> 00:06:07.150
And this is where the continuous
development loop kicks in.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:08.830
So we can see here
below that Cloud Code

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.500
is watching this
code for changes,

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.000
detected that I saved this file,
and starts to automatically

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.810
rebuild, repackage, and
redeploy this up to GKE.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.760
So this code has turned
into a container using

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.120
our Fast container
Builder called jib, which

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.020
knows how to optimally
rebuild and repackage

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.187
a Java application and get
it running in a container

00:06:25.187 --> 00:06:25.970
registry.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:29.560
Right, so this should just
be about to finish up here.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.727
And we can refresh and
see our new message.

00:06:31.727 --> 00:06:33.310
So we're able to
make this simple edit

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.350
and get it running
in GKE very quickly.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.600
This is the basic app, though.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.017
This isn't the
translation backend

00:06:39.017 --> 00:06:40.597
that Jez talked about before.

00:06:40.597 --> 00:06:42.430
So to turn this into a
translation back end,

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:43.780
I'm going to first add
some client libraries

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.113
and enable some APIs so that
we can use the Google Cloud

00:06:46.113 --> 00:06:46.895
Translation API.

00:06:46.895 --> 00:06:49.270
I'm going to do this using
another feature of Cloud Code.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:51.670
I'm going to come in here
and add cloud libraries

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:53.130
and manage APIs.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.640
I'll click that.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.320
This is the translation backend.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.155
So I'm going to click
here on Cloud Translation.

00:06:59.155 --> 00:07:01.030
And there's a feature
to automatically enable

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.360
the API on GCP for us so we
don't have to leave the IDE.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.350
I'll click Continue.

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:08.320
And this next dialog is a way
to have Cloud Code automatically

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.790
create a service
account in secret

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.623
so that you can talk
to this API since we're

00:07:11.623 --> 00:07:13.123
running in Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:07:13.123 --> 00:07:14.635
though, we don't
need to use this.

00:07:14.635 --> 00:07:16.540
I'll click OK.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.040
And now Cloud Code
has automatically

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.750
added this dependency
to our application,

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.880
added it to our
palm.xml, and started

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.946
to rebuild and repackage
this again for us.

00:07:24.946 --> 00:07:28.880
I'm going to switch this code
out for a translation back end.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.780
And while that gets redeployed,
I'll explain how this works.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.030
Here's the main function here.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.127
It takes in some strings
over JSON on the HTTP

00:07:36.127 --> 00:07:37.960
and first passes them
to the translation API

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.942
to detect the incoming language.

00:07:40.942 --> 00:07:43.150
Next, based on what language
it is, we translate this

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.890
to both English and German.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:47.820
And then at the bottom here,
we do some debug logging

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:49.590
to system.out.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:50.440
Awesome.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:51.940
This looks like
it's about finished.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.500
And we can try it out here.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.250
So I have some text
saved that we're going

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.417
to send in as a translation.

00:07:56.417 --> 00:07:58.570
It's a couple strings.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.040
So a couple strings
get passed in--

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:01.598
one in English-- hello world--

00:08:01.598 --> 00:08:02.640
and a sentence in German.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.557
We can see that these
are both translated back

00:08:04.557 --> 00:08:06.610
into English and German.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.730
And the final cool feature is
that cloud code automatically

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.987
streams these logs from
GKE back down to our IDE.

00:08:12.987 --> 00:08:15.570
So we don't need to go anywhere
else to see the debug logging.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.893
JEZ HUMBLE: So not only
did it deploy in Kubernetes

00:08:17.893 --> 00:08:19.310
without you having
to do anything,

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.760
but it also streamed the logs
back into the IDE for you.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.760
And that would have been
hard to do on your own.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:24.490
It's great stuff.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:25.870
SPEAKER 1: Awesome.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.180
So we've got this simple
translation backend running.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.472
Now We're going to work out
how to use this in a bigger

00:08:30.472 --> 00:08:31.550
distributed application.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.169
JEZ HUMBLE: Thanks
very much, Dan.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:33.669
Woo!

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.000
Yeah, live demoing
is terrifying.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.640
So obviously, in a
real system, we're

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.750
going to have more than
one service interacting.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.680
So now we've got our
translation microservice live.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.050
We're going to build
this guest book

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:52.320
and deploy the guest book
which is written in node.js.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.270
And Vijay Vusirikala
is going to show us

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.490
how to do that using VS Code.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.125
take it away, Vijay.

00:08:59.125 --> 00:09:00.000
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: Hi.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.130
This is the-- hi. thanks, Jez.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.630
This is the guest
book application

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.820
that Jez was
mentioning where users

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.940
can add guest book entries in
a language of their choice.

00:09:11.587 --> 00:09:13.920
And we'll go out and integrate
this with the translation

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.010
service that Dan
has built to display

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.180
English and German translations
for these messages.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.190
And I'll be using the cloud
code plugin for VS Code.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.710
And I'll go through one cycle
of the inner development loop,

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.050
make four quick code
changes, deploy them

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.300
to the remote cluster, and
troubleshoot if required.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.570
So the first change, I'll
make it in the display

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:38.260
template to add some style
sheets that are required.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.280
Next, I'll go and add some
place holders for the English

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.330
and German translations.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.240
Then we'll go to the
app.js file and tell it

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.910
that it can find the location
of our translation service

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:53.510
from the Kubernetes
environment variable.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:57.950
Then we'll add the main logic
to call the translation service

00:09:57.950 --> 00:09:59.810
for each of the
guest book entries.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.740
It's a straightforward loop.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.635
We'll add some error
handling as well.

00:10:05.635 --> 00:10:08.260
And then finally, we'll publish
the location of our translation

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.490
end point.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.365
And then we'll go ahead
and save our changes.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.970
And the continuous
deployment has been

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.410
monitoring for your changes.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.960
And once it's detected
them, it's packaged them

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.640
and send it over to the
remote cluster for deployment.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:29.910
And as you can
see, the deployment

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.340
has already finished.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.270
And let's reload this.

00:10:36.073 --> 00:10:36.740
So there you go.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.680
We have our English and
German translations.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.260
That does not look like German.

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:42.410
Does it, Jez?

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.770
JEZ HUMBLE: [SPEAKING GERMAN]

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:46.160
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: OK.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.510
So let's go see
what's happening.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.770
So the logs that were
in the remote cluster

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.960
