00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:08.450
So it must be something going
on with our application.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.640
So why don't we
just try to debug?

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:12.210
Let's set a breakpoint here.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:13.460
And let us start our debugger.

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.110
And the debugger attaches
against a running process

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.710
in the remote cluster.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:24.240
So let's go ahead and
reload our application.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.703
Boom-- the debugger
breakpoint has been hit,

00:11:26.703 --> 00:11:28.120
and we didn't have
to do anything.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.905
This is against
the remote cluster.

00:11:29.905 --> 00:11:31.030
JEZ HUMBLE: That's amazing.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:34.260
So we're debugging using
the regular IDE debugger

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.640
in the Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.450
We didn't have to create
any launch configurations,

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.700
write any YAML, do
any port forwarding.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.530
It just work from
within the IDE.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:44.470
That's awesome stuff.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:52.040
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: And this
is a fully featured debugger.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.690
You saw me stepping through it.

