You can even inspect
the variables.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:01.640
So from this, I can look at
the temporary translations area

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.840
that the code is building.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.550
And I can see that the
German translation is

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.470
reaching my application code.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.870
So maybe we're missing some
kind of assignment statement

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:12.590
somewhere.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.040
So let's go ahead to our
debug console and hard wire

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:18.680
one of these assignments
and see if it gets

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.090
reflected in our application.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.800
So we hardware for
one of the entries.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:31.390
So-- oops-- and let's let
our debugger run through.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:35.300
And there we see.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.190
The entry that we
changed is being

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:38.653
reflected in our application.

00:12:38.653 --> 00:12:40.070
JEZ HUMBLE: And
once again, that's

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.110
changing the state
of the live system

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.090
from within the debugger--

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:45.120
cool stuff.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.640
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: So looks like
we're missing an assignment

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:47.473
statement somewhere.

00:12:47.473 --> 00:12:48.270
There it is.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.720
So let's go ahead and fix it.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.980
And let's save our changes
and stop the debugger.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.260
And Cloud Code would have
picked up these changes

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.330
and would have already
started packaging them

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.480
and started deploying
to the remote cluster.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:05.740
And as soon as it
finishes deployment,

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:06.880
we can reload the page.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:15.730
It should--

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:18.040
JEZ HUMBLE: Obviously, the
IDE takes its time, especially

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:18.730
when there's a demo.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:19.840
That's a built-in feature.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.353
If you're demoing, it
adds a special lag just

00:13:22.353 --> 00:13:23.520
to make your life difficult.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.150
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: So
it will reload the page

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:26.200
with the right entries.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.210
So what you just saw was one
cycle of the inner development

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:29.710
loop.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:30.842
We made some changes.

00:13:30.842 --> 00:13:31.550
We deployed them.

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:33.310
We debugged them,
and you didn't have

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:36.310
to touch any Kubernetes
configurations yourself.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.902
Cloud Code handled all that
for you in the background.

00:13:38.902 --> 00:13:39.610
JEZ HUMBLE: Cool.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:40.300
Thanks very much, Vijay.

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:41.467
VIJAY VUSIRIKALA: Thank you.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:48.380
JEZ HUMBLE: OK, so once
you've got your service live

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:50.810
in production, obviously
you have a whole team

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.070
that takes care of it.

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:54.440
And Ben is going to be
talking about SRE later

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.900
on in this segment.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.380
So one of the key
concepts of SRE

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.910
is SLOs, or Service
Level Objectives.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.830
And these are targets for the
health of your application

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.060
that represents something
a user would experience,

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.810
not a proxy variable like
CPU usage or memory usage.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.790
And once we have
SLOs, what happens

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:16.190
is our application breaks
them, and we get woken up

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:17.970
at 4 o'clock in the morning.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.750
So I've been woken up at
4 o'clock in the morning

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.900
because our application
has fallen below its SLOs,

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:25.850
and I'm going to ping
Christie Wilson, who

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:27.703
is on call for this app.

00:14:27.703 --> 00:14:29.120
And she's going
to work out what's

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.318
going on so that we
can remediate and get

00:14:31.318 --> 00:14:32.360
everything working again.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:34.190
Over to you, Christie.

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.680
CHRISTIE WILSON: All
right, so it's 4:00 AM.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:38.882
I'm on call, and
I've been paged.

00:14:38.882 --> 00:14:41.090
I need to figure out what's
going wrong in production

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:42.960
and fix it as
quickly as possible.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:47.290
So to do that, I'm going to
use Cloud Service Mesh, or CSM.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:50.630
So CSM is currently in
alpha, and it requires Istio.

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.550
But out of the box,
I can take a look

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:56.710
at things about my running
services like the metrics.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:15:00.330
I can look at the logs, and
I can look at other telemetry

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.530
data as well.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:06.850
And I can create
my own custom SLOs.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.460
So here, we can see
that I've already

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.370
defined one for 95th
percentile latency,

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.290
and it's being violated.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.450
And since I'm looking at
the front end service,

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.850
I have a pretty good guess
that the reason I was paged

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.860
is because the front end
service is overloaded.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.590
So I need to mitigate
this as fast as possible

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:24.960
so I can get back to bed.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:28.080
So to do that, I'm
going to use Cloud Code.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.480
So Cloud Code can be used
by all kinds of engineers,

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.160
whether you're
wearing your developer

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:33.998
hat or your operations hat.

00:15:33.998 --> 00:15:35.790
And so what I'm going
to do is I'm actually

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.460
going to take a look directly
at the production cluster

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.320
in the Cluster Explorer.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.090
Now, obviously,
not all developers

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:45.630
should have access to this,
but I'm on call, so I do.

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:47.400
So in the Cluster
Explorer, I can

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:49.560
start looking at what's
going on in production.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:51.240
I can see my deployments.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.620
They're all green.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:55.950
And I can start diving
into individual pods.

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:58.360
I can look at the
containers in those pods,

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.330
and I can see details
like what environment

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:03.270
variables they're running
with, what the status is.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.900
And I can even get the logs
from my production cluster

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.730
right in my IDE.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.720
And if I want to, I can
even connect a terminal

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.330
and start executing commands
in my production containers.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.330
So one thing that I'm
noticing as I look at this

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:23.720
is that our overloaded service
only has one replica running.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:25.750
And since it's overloaded,
I'm thinking maybe

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.760
a way I can work around
this is to increase

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:29.530
the number of replicas.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:31.840
So to do that, I'm going
to use this edit icon here,

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:34.990
and I'm actually going to bring
up the production configuration

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:38.300
right in my IDE
and make a change.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:42.950
So I want to increase the
number of replicas from 1 to 10.

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:46.810
But I'm tired, so I made a typo,
and I put the value in wrong.

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:49.430
But fortunately,
the IDE has my back.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:51.670
So it's telling me, because
it's aware of the schema,

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.290
that the type is wrong here
and this should be an integer.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.590
So I can make the
change I meant to.

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:58.540
And because it is
aware of the schema,

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:01.450
the IDE, can give me
IntelliSense and show me

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:05.630
what values are valid at this
point in the configuration.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:07.359
So let's go ahead and save that.

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:09.536
And the IDE prompts
me to warn me

00:17:09.536 --> 00:17:11.619
that it's about to apply
this live to a production

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:14.630
cluster which, yes,
I did want to do.

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:17.410
And in the Cluster
Explorer I can refresh.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:20.050
And I can see that
Kubernetes is immediately

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:22.839
doing what it does best and
scaling out the 10 replicas

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:24.970
that I requested.

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.814
JEZ HUMBLE: So any
ops people, SREs,

00:17:27.814 --> 00:17:30.040
or sysadmins are probably
having a mild heart

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.080
attack at the moment
watching Christie

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:34.210
change the state of a
live production cluster

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:35.717
from her IDE.

00:17:35.717 --> 00:17:37.300
And in fairness,
that wasn't a feature

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.040
we'd originally planned.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.350
We got feedback from
our early access users

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.150
that they wanted
this functionality.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.040
And in fairness,
it's super useful

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:47.650
if you're managing a dev
cluster or a test cluster.

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.130
It's really, really helpful.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.280
However, in real
life, what we want

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:53.740
to do is make this change
to version control,

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.810
push it out through a
deployment pipeline.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:57.820
This is the paradigm
known as infrastructure

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:59.440
as code or GitOps.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.340
So Christie, how
would we do that?

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:03.007
CHRISTIE WILSON: Well,
let's take a look

00:18:03.007 --> 00:18:05.125
at the files in version control.

00:18:05.125 --> 00:18:07.000
So this is the deployment
in version control.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.370
And as you can see, because
of my emergency change,

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:11.500
it has fallen out of
sync with production.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.120
So I'm going to make
the change here.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.620
But before I do
that, I can show you

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:17.315
some other Cloud Code features.

00:18:17.315 --> 00:18:18.940
If you've been working
with Kubernetes,

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.565
you've probably
noticed that you end up

00:18:20.565 --> 00:18:22.390
dealing with a lot of YAML.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:25.210
But having YAML support
directly in your IDE

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:27.440
can make that a
lot less painful.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.980
For example, if I'm looking
at this Mongo deployment

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.890
and say I want to know what
the value is of this secret,

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:35.945
instead of having to dig
around in the YAML in my repo

00:18:35.945 --> 00:18:37.570
and figure out where
that secret lives,

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.700
I can actually just
peek at it directly

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:41.800
and see what the value is.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:44.660
And these base64
encoded strings,

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.300
I can just hover
over them and see

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:47.890
what the plain text values are.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:49.020
JEZ HUMBLE: I just
want to emphasize,

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:50.620
Christie did not
choose that password.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:51.973
Christie would never do that.

00:18:51.973 --> 00:18:53.640
CHRISTIE WILSON: Yeah,
I'm going to have

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.960
some words with some
people after this, I think.

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:59.230
So what I'm going to do now
is open the configuration

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:01.240
and make the change
to 10 replicas.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:03.540
So now, I would
commit this, push it,

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:04.960
and open a pull request.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.700
And then Cloud
Build would kick in.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.283
And it would do all
the things that we

00:19:08.283 --> 00:19:10.720
want to do to properly land
this change in production,

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:15.260
like running vulnerability
scanning, tests, et cetera.

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:17.082
JEZ HUMBLE: Thank you
so much, Christie.

00:19:17.082 --> 00:19:19.968
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:25.140
And so this is what it would
look like in Google Cloud

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.900
Platform in Cloud Build,
seeing the builds that

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:32.010
have gone through into
prods from version control.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:34.440
So once we've got
everything working again,

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:36.270
we're going to go back
to sleep, hopefully,

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.160
and get up in the
morning and work out

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.470
what the actual problem was
in the app that was causing

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:41.970
that performance degradation.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.540
We can use Stackdriver
Profiler to do that.

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:46.530
Stackdriver Profiler
is really powerful.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.900
Some of our customers have
seen double digit reductions

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:51.150
in CPU usage as a
result of using.

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:55.410
It and it's generally
available as of Tuesday.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:58.290
So today, we showed
you Cloud Code.

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:01.440
Cloud Code lets see you
develop, deploy, and debug

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.030
your Kubernetes-based
applications

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.010
directly from within your IDE.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:06.990
And it doesn't just
work against GKE.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.310
It works against any
Kubernetes cluster.

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:12.870
Whether that's local, in a data
center, another cloud provider,

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:16.450
Cloud Code will talk to
any Kubernetes cluster.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:18.700
You can download the code
that we've showed you today.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:22.250
It's available in nodes, in
Go, in Java, and in Python.

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:23.910
Start playing with
that right now.

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:28.088
And we really hope you're as
excited as we are about this

00:20:28.088 --> 00:20:30.630
powerful functionality, and we
cannot wait to see what you do

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:31.200
with it.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:32.610
Thanks very much for your time.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:33.360
Back to you, Pali.

00:20:42.617 --> 00:20:43.700
PALI BHAT: Thank you, Jez.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:46.640
That was super
exciting to see and one

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:49.160
of the great ways you can
increase developer productivity

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:52.530
while you're building cloud
native Kubernetes applications.

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:57.930
So let's talk about the next
thing, which is shifting left.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:01.110
And the purpose of shifting
left, as we discussed earlier,

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:04.910
is to help connect all of
the downstream feedback

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:07.640
to your developers and
to your development teams

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:12.860
so you can catch those costly
errors much, much sooner.

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:15.440
This could be everything
from enforcing

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:19.040
coding guidelines to getting
operational metrics earlier

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:20.150
in the cycle.

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:23.270
And of course, one of the most
important things you want to do

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:27.320
is catch those security errors
very, very early in the process

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:30.560
because those can
be really costly.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.600
To tell you a little
bit more about how

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.390
we're thinking about
shifting security left

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:37.970
and give you a
practitioner's view of it,

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:40.830
I want to welcome Mike
Berry from ANZ onstage.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:52.180
MIKE BERRY: So good
morning, everyone.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:53.310
My name is Mike.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:56.430
And yes, I work at a bank.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:00.150
The ANZ Bank has been operating
in Australia for the past 180

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:03.270
years, and every day, we
look after the details

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:05.380
and information of
millions of customers.

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:07.990
And we look after billions
of dollars of transactions.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:11.340
So as you can imagine, security
is very important to the way

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.750
that we operate.

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:17.380
Now, banking over those 180
years has changed considerably.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:20.490
It used to be about how secure
your vault was in your branch,

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:21.968
and that was security.

00:22:21.968 --> 00:22:23.760
But now we have more
things to think about.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.052
In particular, today we're
going to talk about security

00:22:26.052 --> 00:22:28.080
of our services and
applications as they're

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.840
running in the cloud.

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:34.913
Now we have
vulnerability scanning.

00:22:34.913 --> 00:22:36.580
So we're going to
talk about two things.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:38.670
The first,
vulnerability scanning--

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.747
when you're running a
vulnerability scanning,

00:22:40.747 --> 00:22:42.330
the question is, how
are you doing it?

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:44.370
So today, do you have--

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.830
in the audience here, do you
have vulnerability scanning

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:48.722
of some kind that you're
running right now?

00:22:48.722 --> 00:22:49.680
Who has their hands up?

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.080
Yeah, we have-- all right, we
have vulnerability scanners

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:52.580
out here.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:53.520
This is very good.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.800
My question is, when
you run these scans,

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:57.610
what do you do with them?

00:22:57.610 --> 00:22:59.610
Do you go, oh, I've
got this scan run,

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:01.920
and now I'm going
to raise a ticket.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.070
Or I've got this report,
and I'll look into that

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:05.580
or raise a risk or--

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.288
do these things last for
a day or for a few days?

00:23:08.288 --> 00:23:10.080
Or sometimes, you're
like, oh, my goodness.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:11.760
We haven't run this
for a few weeks.

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.760
And now we're getting to
the end of the project.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:14.700
Quick, run a scan.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:15.367
See how it goes.

00:23:15.367 --> 00:23:16.950
And then, oh, my
goodness, we're going

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:19.680
to spend days going through
these vulnerabilities doing

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.950
patching, doing
retesting, doing re-fixes.

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:25.410
And then finally,
it's all sorted out.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:29.640
And so that kind of
approach to your security

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.920
can really slow down your
work and impede your velocity.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:34.890
So what we're doing
now at the ANZ

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:38.460
is we're using the vulnerability
scanner in Google Cloud.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:41.650
But we're using it in
every build that we do.

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:46.580
So we see here we
have our source code.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.600
Our source code moves
into Cloud Build

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:50.760
and does all the usual
things we do in a build.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:53.650
But at the same time, we
run that vulnerability scan.

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:56.860
And what happens is if that
vulnerability scan fails,

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:58.680
then instantly, we
have a red build.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:00.960
And a developer can go
in, have a look at that,

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:02.560
find out what is the issue.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:04.440
And it will highlight
for you the severity.

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:07.680
It will highlight for you which
package is impacted by that.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.320
And you can go in and you can
do the patching or remediation,

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:11.520
whatever is required.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:12.290
And you fix that.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:14.100
So you find that problem.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.170
You fix that one small problem.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:17.400
And then that's resolved.

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:18.542
Run the build again.

00:24:18.542 --> 00:24:20.500
Everything's green and
everything's fine again.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.830
And so instead of taking
this huge amount of pain

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:27.095
down the end of weeks of
your work and slowing down,

00:24:27.095 --> 00:24:29.220
we just take these little
increments along the way.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:31.200
When you find these
things as they occur,

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:32.580
we fix them straightaway.

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:34.860
And that helps us keep that
velocity moving as we're

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:36.880
working in our development.

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:41.190
So that's one way that
we're approaching security.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:43.770
The other one is
binary authorization.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:46.740
So we've looked at kind of the
left side and that CI process.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:49.200
Now we need to move
on to the deployments

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.000
so as we move our
deployment through,

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:54.570
how are we doing the
enforcement of this security?

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:57.360
Because you may have lots
of security applications

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.610
because that's the--

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.470
when we scan things
for vulnerabilities,

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:02.820
that's one process we have.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:04.860
But we have lots of
other security tools,

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:06.600
lots of other test processes.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.250
Maybe you have
performance testing.

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:10.380
How are you getting
that information

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:13.140
and making sure that it
applies to that binary

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:15.600
that you're moving into your
production environments?

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:18.397
Because if you have security
and you have lots of reports

00:25:18.397 --> 00:25:19.980
and you leave those
reports stacked up

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:22.710
in a big pile on your desk,
that's not helping anyone.

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:25.470
What you need to do with that
information is enforce that.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:29.920
And so binary authorization is
a way to force your security.

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:32.310
Now I will talk
about a little bit,

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:35.460
but maybe what we'll do first
is we'll do a little demo

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:39.600
and have a look at
how this applies.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:42.000
All right, there's no
shortage of laptops today.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.010
All right, here you go.

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:47.030
So this is a simple demo.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:50.210
What we've got here
is my two images

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:51.800
in my Container Registry.

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:53.420
And now by looking
at those images,

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:56.510
you tell me which one has
passed all of my testing and all

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.530
my criteria and
which one has not.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:01.700
And the answer is
you can't tell.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:02.643
Just say no.

00:26:02.643 --> 00:26:03.810
But we're going to find out.

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:05.227
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:26:05.227 --> 00:26:07.070
going to grab the
business processor,

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:08.750
and we're going to deploy it.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:13.050
And one button deploys.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.100
Yes, and we're going
to hit Refresh.

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.190
We're going to look
at our cluster.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.170
And oh, my goodness--

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:20.680
that's the wrong one.

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:23.130
So what we see here
is that this is

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:26.850
being forbidden by a policy
that says this image has not

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:27.510
been approved.

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:30.180
And so the way this works,
binary authorization sees

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.880
your image, comes in, and it
looks for an attachment that's

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:34.060
attached to this.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:36.150
So that's a way to attach
a certificate to say,

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:38.153
this has passed our policies.

00:26:38.153 --> 00:26:39.570
And we have a
certificate to prove

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.640
that this image is OK with us.

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:43.615
And in the case of
this particular image,

00:26:43.615 --> 00:26:45.240
that image is not
OK, and it should not

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.340
be deployed to our environment.

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.030
Now, don't worry.

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:50.633
We're not going to
leave it with red.

00:26:50.633 --> 00:26:52.800
We're going to have a look
at Business Orchestrator.

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:54.250
That's our other image.

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:57.050
I'm going to press a
button, Orchestrator.

00:26:57.050 --> 00:26:58.800
I'm going to press that button.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:01.410
Magic is going to happen.

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:02.705
Yes.

00:27:02.705 --> 00:27:05.070
Hit Refresh.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:07.170
It's a perfect demo.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:09.290
So green lights, everybody.

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.730
We're all happy because
this image actually

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:13.470
has that certificate
attached to it.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:14.940
Binary authorization reads it.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:16.590
It says this has
passed your policies.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:18.160
You are cleared all
the way through.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.060
And you've got a big
green light for that.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:23.790
And so the way it
works is imagine this.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.232
You have an internal audit team.

00:27:26.232 --> 00:27:27.690
And they come to
you, and they say,

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.790
we need to understand
your controls.

00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:31.600
We need to understand
your policies.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:33.420
So what we're going to
do with you is we're

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.462
going to set you a meeting
for a couple of hours.

00:27:35.462 --> 00:27:37.620
And we're going to read
documentation together.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:40.530
And we're going to understand
how your policies work.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:42.840
You're like, well, that sounds
like a lot of fun, guys.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:45.070
But how about we do a demo?

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:46.390
I've got an image here.

00:27:46.390 --> 00:27:48.690
This image has been
approved by nobody.

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:50.400
It's been tested by nobody.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.562
And really, I don't even
know where it came from.

00:27:52.562 --> 00:27:54.270
And I'm going to deploy
it to production.

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:55.780
Then you jump into production.

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:57.120
You press the Deploy button.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.580
And you show your
audit team, look,

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:01.470
this cannot be
deployed to production.

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.420
We don't just talk
about security here.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:05.025
We enforce our security.

00:28:05.025 --> 00:28:06.900
And so we make sure
these things move through

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:08.310
because they're approved.

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:10.470
Now, when I've done
this demo, I just

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:13.200
did that with the one
tester, the one policy.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.940
But you can have a
multitude of policies

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:17.840
that you might want to apply and
do that for different clusters

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.290
depending on how you set
up your organization.

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:21.960
So imagine in one
cluster, you're like,

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.390
all that has to happen here
is we need a peer review.

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:25.380
That's all we need.

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:26.940
You can deploy it
in this cluster.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.370
The production cluster,
maybe you want a peer review.

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:31.800
Maybe you want to pass a
certain phase of testing.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:34.000
And maybe you want to add
to that there's a team,

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.100
or even a person if you choose
to, that is a part of this.

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.320
So if we go back
to the slides now,

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:44.170
we can see on the left side,
we have our vulnerability

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:44.670
scanning.

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:46.330
We're testing these
things straight away.

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:48.205
And then as they move
through to deployments,

00:28:48.205 --> 00:28:51.090
we're making sure that we have
just those trusted images that

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:53.430
move through and they're
deployed to our engine.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:56.790
And because this is all a
pipeline, so we have security.

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:59.910
But it's moving through with
automation and with the speed

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:02.440
that we have available from
this automated pipeline.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:03.990
And so at the ANZ
Bank, that's how

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.300
we're applying these two
principles to make sure

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:09.540
that we move with security and
all the things that we need

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:13.460
but moving that security forward
with speed and reliability.

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:14.340
So thank you.

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:15.150
Back to you, Pali.

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:29.970
PALI BHAT: So we talked about
how we can increase developer

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:32.110
productivity with Cloud Code.

00:29:32.110 --> 00:29:35.100
We then talked about
shifting left, especially

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.700
on security with Cloud Build.

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:42.900
Now let's talk about how you
can scale your operations.

00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:47.820
This is one of the huge benefits
of Cloud and why all of you

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:51.260
are adopting cloud today.

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:53.630
So as we think about
scaled operations,

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:57.920
my mind goes back to
Google because we've

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:02.880
got eight products with
over 1 billion users each.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:06.140
And as you saw during
the keynote yesterday,

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:09.140
Urs shared a study
that showed that of all

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:14.340
of the major clouds, Google
Cloud was the most reliable.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:19.640
And much of that is because
we have internally in Google

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:24.740
an amazing SRE team that
helps us scale and operate

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:27.510
all of these Google
products and, of course,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.980
Google Cloud as well.

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:32.420
And for the last 15
plus years, there's

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:35.100
been one person at
the head of that team.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.450
So I'd love to welcome on
board Ben Treynor, who's

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:41.200
been leading our SRE team
for the last 15 years,

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:42.050
for a fireside chat.

00:30:48.888 --> 00:30:50.180
BEN TREYNOR: No pressure, then.

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:57.370
PALI BHAT: So Ben,
we talked about all

00:30:57.370 --> 00:30:59.350
of these billion user services.

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:01.230
BEN TREYNOR: Yes.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.970
PALI BHAT: I wonder how
many folks in the audience

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:07.180
are using billion user
services every day.

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:11.530
BEN TREYNOR: Well, I suspect,
you know, probably a few.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:13.920
But it's also-- it's one
of those interesting myths.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:16.540
You say, well, SRE,
isn't that for only the

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:18.920
really, really,
really big services?

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:22.330
And I think we maybe have
an audience-- an opportunity

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:23.630
to talk about that a bit.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:27.170
But certainly, you know, I
started the SRE team a little,

00:31:27.170 --> 00:31:29.560
as you say, 15 and
1/2 years ago now.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:31.630
Google was not that
big at that point.

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:34.780
And in particular, some of the
services that we were running

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:37.867
were actually fairly
modest in size.

00:31:37.867 --> 00:31:40.450
And we've found that we have a
set of principles and practices

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:45.010
that we can use across services
at pretty much any scale.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:46.570
And we found that they helped.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:48.850
PALI BHAT: Yeah.

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.610
So Ben, as you think
about these SRE practices,

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:54.980
could you share a little bit
more about what those are?

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:56.690
BEN TREYNOR: Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:56.690 --> 00:32:00.130
And I think you have a slide up
here that talks about a study

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:03.880
that Forester did recently
about SRE practices

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:07.270
and said, essentially, you know,
can these be used by anybody,

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:09.290
or do you need this
huge monolith of code

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:10.290
and engineering support?

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:12.880
And they found that roughly
half of them did not need this.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.820
But let me talk about a few of
the SRE practices that I think

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:18.370
are usable and applicable
to pretty much anybody

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:20.570
who's running a service.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:23.290
The first is one that we talked
about a bit earlier, which

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:24.370
is having SLOs.

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:26.530
And SLO is a pretty dry term.

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:28.390
It stands for a Service
Level Objective,

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:29.925
and, you know, the
number of people

00:32:29.925 --> 00:32:33.700
who get excited about service
level objectives, it's modest,

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:36.230
let me assure you.

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:39.700
But what SLOs really
mean is you want

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:44.200
to be able to measure what your
actual end user experience is,

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:48.730
and you want to have an
opinion about what performance

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:51.310
would be sufficient
for most of your users

00:32:51.310 --> 00:32:53.000
to like your product.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:56.000
We actually internally
call them happy SLOs.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:57.680
It sounds very cheery.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:00.110
But it's a very
simple concept, right?

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:03.010
Measure your application
the way your end user

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:06.250
sees it and have an opinion
about what the end user would

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:08.650
need to see to be happy.

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:10.360
Sounds really easy.

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:12.460
It's actually usually
fairly hard in practice.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:15.940
Most applications don't actually
instrument what the user sees.

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:18.980
They instrument some things
that are along the way and say,

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:21.080
well, good enough, right?

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:24.880
And what we've found in SRE and
in running Google services is

00:33:24.880 --> 00:33:27.370
actually, that last
mile of instrumenting

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.760
the common activities that
users are actually doing

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:31.790
is really important.

00:33:31.790 --> 00:33:36.070
Oftentimes, the behavior that
they see isn't what you think,

00:33:36.070 --> 00:33:39.820
and the instrumentation that
you do on intermediate steps

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:42.040
doesn't actually tell you
about some of the problems

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.450
that you've got.

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:45.580
And so I like to
differentiate and say,

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:47.070
like, there's black
box monitoring,

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.590
and black box monitoring is
what your happy SLOs should

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:51.150
eventually should be looking at.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:53.950
Now, as we saw earlier,
if you're on call

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:57.070
and the monitor says
something's wrong,

00:33:57.070 --> 00:33:59.020
you want a little more
information than that.

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:00.562
And so in addition
to that, you know,

00:34:00.562 --> 00:34:04.060
you'll clearly have SLOs for
internal microservices and SLOs

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:05.470
for intermediate
steps and so on.

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.960
But the most important thing is
instrumenting the end result,

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:10.293
because after all, that's the
only thing your user sees.

00:34:10.293 --> 00:34:12.699
And really, it's the only
thing that they care about.

00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:14.699
And that's something that
you can do right away.

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:17.020
In fact, really, it's
easier, trust me,

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:21.850
to do when the application is
small than when it gets bigger.

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:25.179
So another concept that we use
that really sort of applies

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:28.030
to everyone is this
concept of error budgets.

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:31.480
And an error budget sounds
like a very sort of odd thing.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.860
If you've read the SRE book,
it probably seems less odd.

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:38.170
But it's an important concept
in site reliability, which

00:34:38.170 --> 00:34:42.550
is that our goal is actually
not perfect availability

00:34:42.550 --> 00:34:48.199
because, number one, right,
your users are using a device--

00:34:48.199 --> 00:34:52.600
maybe a laptop maybe a
phone, maybe a computer--

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:54.364
which is not 100% reliable.

00:34:54.364 --> 00:34:55.989
They're communicating
with your service

00:34:55.989 --> 00:34:59.317
over a network which
is not 100% reliable,

00:34:59.317 --> 00:35:00.650
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:35:00.650 --> 00:35:02.358
And so the difference
between, let's say,

00:35:02.358 --> 00:35:06.250
five nines and perfection
is probably not

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:08.440
distinguishable by your
users because there are so

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:11.020
many other things in
the path that will also

00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:12.520
break their experience.

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:15.910
But the engineering cost of the
difference between five nines

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:19.120
and perfect is immense, and its
drag on your feature velocity

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:20.200
is immense.

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.098
And ultimately, you're
running a business.

00:35:22.098 --> 00:35:23.140
You're running a service.

00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:25.600
And so these two
basic goals, I want

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:27.880
to service that's
reliable but I also

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:29.200
want high feature velocity--

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:31.720
I want to be able to launch
new things that my users want--

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:32.590
is really important.

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:36.220
And error budgets is the concept
that lets you bridge those two.

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:38.380
And it's really fairly simple.

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:40.720
If your availability
target is four nines,

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:44.590
then your error budget
is that last 0.01%.

00:35:44.590 --> 00:35:48.550
And you can spend it
silly errors, right?

00:35:48.550 --> 00:35:50.290
Pushes that shouldn't
have been made,

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:52.090
features that
basically don't work.

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:54.610
Or you can spend it taking
calculated risk, right?

00:35:54.610 --> 00:35:57.850
Exposing a new feature
to 0.01% or 0.1%

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:00.730
of your users for 10 minutes
and measuring the result.

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:05.020
And if you have happy SLOs, you
know whether it worked or not.

00:36:05.020 --> 00:36:07.090
This lets you have very
fast feature velocity

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:08.890
because you can
take a small risk,

00:36:08.890 --> 00:36:10.810
look at its effect on
your SLO, and decide

00:36:10.810 --> 00:36:13.090
whether you want to make
that a larger risk or not.

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:17.440
And it also helps the SRE
team or your operations team

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:19.240
agree with your
development team.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:20.005
Should we do this?

00:36:20.005 --> 00:36:21.130
Well, are we out of budget?

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:22.040
Yes or no?

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:24.245
If we're not out of
budget, let's go ahead

00:36:24.245 --> 00:36:25.120
and launch it, right?

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:26.150
We have budget to spare.

00:36:26.150 --> 00:36:27.733
And if we are out
of budget, well, OK.

00:36:27.733 --> 00:36:30.640
Data says we're not making
sufficiently prudent or careful

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:33.160
choices and we should hold
back and spend a bit more time

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:36.040
on availability.

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:37.965
Blameless
Post-Mortems-- somebody

00:36:37.965 --> 00:36:39.340
mentioned it
earlier when I think

00:36:39.340 --> 00:36:41.203
there was a hiccup in a demo.

00:36:41.203 --> 00:36:43.120
This is an SRE practice
that's actually pretty

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:44.470
easy for everyone to adopt.

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:48.130
And it's not a
complicated idea--

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:50.620
again, in theory, harder
to implement in practice.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:53.650
But the basic idea here is
you've hired good people.

00:36:53.650 --> 00:36:56.188
You have a recruiting
process to identify

00:36:56.188 --> 00:36:58.480
people who are intelligent
and smart, well-intentioned.

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:01.840
And despite all of that, despite
these people doing their best,

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:04.030
something went wrong.

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:07.060
Look at how your system
works and figure out

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:09.610
how you'll make it less
likely for the system

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:12.580
to break, easier for the people
who are looking after it to fix

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:16.150
it when it breaks,
and make those

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:17.650
the goals of a
post-mortem, right?

00:37:17.650 --> 00:37:20.050
Figure out how to make
it more likely to succeed

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:22.020
next time despite the
fact that there are going

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:23.520
to be people working
on this who are

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:27.460
intelligent and
well-intentioned and imperfect.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:30.928
And then finally, capping
and eliminating toil--

00:37:30.928 --> 00:37:32.470
this is actually--
it's better if you

00:37:32.470 --> 00:37:34.610
start this at a small scale.

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:38.620
But this is the idea that
every system, as time passes,

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:40.690
requires more and
more things that

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:44.227
need to be done by hand because,
you know, we were in a rush.

00:37:44.227 --> 00:37:46.060
You needed to get that
feature out the door,

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:48.518
and there were a few bits that
weren't fully buttoned down.

00:37:48.518 --> 00:37:49.870
But we can deal with them later.

00:37:49.870 --> 00:37:52.253
My experience is there is
never the later, right?

00:37:52.253 --> 00:37:53.170
There is only the now.

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:54.790
And by the time you
finish that feature,

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:56.110
there will be the
next round of features

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.610
that you're already
slightly behind.

00:37:57.610 --> 00:37:59.800
So you know, get
rid of that fantasy

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:04.033
and instead say, toil, you
know, human work to do something

00:38:04.033 --> 00:38:05.950
where that work does not
make tomorrow better.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:07.300
You'll just have to do it again.

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:11.140
Toil is another budget
that you have to manage.

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:15.102
And in SRE at Google, we
say toil is capped at 50%.

00:38:15.102 --> 00:38:17.560
You can't have the team spending
more than half of its time

00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:19.450
on sort of non-repeatable
human actions

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:23.020
to support various interesting
features and snowflakes and so

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:24.520
on.

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:26.403
And the rest of
the time has to be

00:38:26.403 --> 00:38:28.570
spent making the service
better, building production

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:29.805
infrastructure, et cetera.

00:38:29.805 --> 00:38:31.180
And once you view
it as a budget,

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:32.597
then you ask the
question of where

00:38:32.597 --> 00:38:35.020
am I going to spend
my toil budget.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:37.523
And it'll be on the things that
are brand new and hard where

00:38:37.523 --> 00:38:39.940
you don't really know exactly
how to do them automatically

00:38:39.940 --> 00:38:40.990
yet.

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:43.323
And then you'll have a fraction
of your development time

00:38:43.323 --> 00:38:45.040
that's spent implementing
and deploying

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:47.200
projects that get rid
of toil in the places

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:49.120
where it's now easier.

00:38:49.120 --> 00:38:51.850
And that cycle kind
of continues forever.

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:54.970
But it's important to keep
this as an explicit budget,

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.000
and it's important
to actually watch

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:59.417
to make sure your teams don't
spend too much of their time

00:38:59.417 --> 00:39:01.600
on toil because there's
only two options, right?

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:04.510
Option number one, your
service is not successful.

00:39:04.510 --> 00:39:07.450
Your business goes under and
none of this matters, right?

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:09.760
Option two, your
service is successful.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:11.720
It grows, and toil
typically grows

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:13.720
roughly linearly with the
size of your business.

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:16.392
So if your team is 50%
occupied with toil right now

00:39:16.392 --> 00:39:18.850
and not doing anything to fix
it and your business doubles,

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:20.980
guess what, right?

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:24.220
You're going to be hiring
a bunch more SREs--

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:25.610
not a good plan.

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:27.923
So this is, in some
ways, a plan for success,

00:39:27.923 --> 00:39:30.340
and it involves a certain
amount of continuous investment.

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:33.160
But it absolutely
is the right path

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.690
if you plan to be successful.

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:37.480
PALI BHAT: So Ben, as
I talk to customers,

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:40.690
the first thing they tell me is,
like, they love this concept.

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:42.620
They want to implement all this.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:46.630
But they're worried it will
take them a lot of money,

00:39:46.630 --> 00:39:49.930
cost them a lot of money, or
they probably need new people.

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:52.023
So how do you respond to them?

00:39:52.023 --> 00:39:53.190
BEN TREYNOR: First, I laugh.

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:56.260
Ha ha ha.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:59.380
So you know, as I tell
people, I'm actually

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:00.700
a software engineer, right?

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:03.040
This is the first
operationally oriented role

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:04.618
I've had in my entire career.

00:40:04.618 --> 00:40:06.160
I started out in
enterprise software,

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:09.250
interestingly, at Oracle when
they were a little 400 person

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:09.760
company.

00:40:09.760 --> 00:40:11.380
Who knew?

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:15.040
And you don't actually
need new people to do SRE

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:17.330
or to do DevOps or to
do these practices.

00:40:17.330 --> 00:40:20.413
In fact, you should have
people who are engineers,

00:40:20.413 --> 00:40:22.330
and who consider themselves
software engineers

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:23.770
and who will be
bored to tears if you

00:40:23.770 --> 00:40:25.812
ask them to do the same
thing over and over again

00:40:25.812 --> 00:40:28.870
because they have the right
mindset to say, how do I make

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:32.710
a machine do all of this boring
work, this repetitive work,

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:35.380
this vigilance work that people
are actually quite bad at,

00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:37.330
so that I can get on to
doing something that's

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:38.050
more interesting?

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:39.300
And that's a great motivation.

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:42.310
These practices then
give them guidance

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:44.687
on how to do that effectively.

00:40:44.687 --> 00:40:46.270
You definitely don't
need new tooling.

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:48.980
I mean, all of the things that
we've described here-- like,

00:40:48.980 --> 00:40:52.850
what you have now in cloud
is way better than what we

00:40:52.850 --> 00:40:54.780
had in SRE in the early days.

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:57.080
And in fact, a number of the
systems that we have here

00:40:57.080 --> 00:40:59.870
have been influenced by
what people have discovered

00:40:59.870 --> 00:41:02.660
in DevOps over the years.

00:41:02.660 --> 00:41:04.820
And yes, this isn't just
for cloud native, right?

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:08.000
These same rules of
physics essentially

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:09.210
apply to any system.

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:11.780
I will say that services
that are running in the cloud

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:14.420
definitely have it easier
because as you've just shown,

00:41:14.420 --> 00:41:15.950
you've built a
bunch of the things

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:18.808
into cloud that we had
to build ourselves.

00:41:18.808 --> 00:41:21.350
But the open source community,
if you're still running things

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:22.808
in-house, the open
source community

00:41:22.808 --> 00:41:25.260
now offers a lot of these
systems for you as well.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:28.110
And I would definitely
take advantage of them.

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:30.290
PALI BHAT: So as I talk
to these customers again,

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:32.880
the other common
thing I hear is, hey,

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:34.452
we've already got a DevOps team.

00:41:34.452 --> 00:41:35.160
BEN TREYNOR: Yep.

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:40.790
PALI BHAT: And so do we need to
now create a second SRE team?

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:42.590
BEN TREYNOR: So the
good news is no.

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:45.570
I've gotten this question
a few times over the years.

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:51.280
I coined SRE back in 2004, and
there was DevOps at that time

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:51.780
as well.

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:56.540
But both of them have
emerged as industry,

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:59.390
let's call them,
tracks since then.

00:41:59.390 --> 00:42:02.150
But SRE and DevOps
are not different.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.960
SRE is a particular
instantiation of ops

00:42:05.960 --> 00:42:06.680
but nothing more.

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:09.260
And if you actually
go through and read

00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:11.300
what the different
branches of DevOps

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:13.880
have to say about how
systems should be managed,

00:42:13.880 --> 00:42:16.460
how services should be
monitored, et cetera,

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:18.440
they're very similar
to what SRE does.

00:42:18.440 --> 00:42:22.370
I simply would say SRE is a
more specific implementation

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:24.860
of this general class of DevOps.

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:26.540
It's worked well for us.

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:29.090
It doesn't necessarily have
to be exactly the recipe

00:42:29.090 --> 00:42:30.020
that everyone follows.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:31.570
It's not the only
path to success.

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:34.070
But I would say these two are
very much complementary ideas.

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:37.735
And in some places I expect,
the folks in the broader DevOps

00:42:37.735 --> 00:42:39.110
community absolutely
will come up

00:42:39.110 --> 00:42:43.751
with a superior approach, in
which case we will adopt it.

00:42:43.751 --> 00:42:47.510
And we have written now
two books in the SRE team

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:49.940
covering our specific
implementation.

00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:53.000
And again, I think those are
useful for folks to understand

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:54.680
what we did and how we did it.

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:57.230
They don't necessarily have
to be adopted wholesale

00:42:57.230 --> 00:42:58.410
by other teams.

00:42:58.410 --> 00:42:59.600
PALI BHAT: Yeah.

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:03.410
So Ben, could you actually
share with the audience

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:06.710
what are like a few things
that they could just

00:43:06.710 --> 00:43:07.732
start doing tomorrow--

00:43:07.732 --> 00:43:08.440
BEN TREYNOR: Yes.

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:09.232
PALI BHAT: --right?

00:43:09.232 --> 00:43:09.774
To adopt SRE.

00:43:09.774 --> 00:43:12.190
BEN TREYNOR: So interestingly,
when we wrote the first SRE

00:43:12.190 --> 00:43:14.360
book, that was one of the
principal questions we got

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:16.073
back is, OK, this
all sounds great,

00:43:16.073 --> 00:43:17.490
and these are
interesting stories.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:20.195
But how do I turn
this into practice?

00:43:20.195 --> 00:43:21.820
And so we wrote the
second book in part

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:25.130
to answer that question, and if
you have the time and energy,

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:26.690
I'd definitely
recommend reading it.

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:28.430
We put a fair bit into it.

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:32.000
But let me give you three
that you can do today

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:35.640
or immediately that are three
of the most important practices,

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:36.890
I think.

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:39.500
The first is to
actually write down

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:42.080
those happy SLOs
we talked about.

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:47.150
It is perhaps unsurprising
but still surprising

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:51.265
how few SLOs actually
get defined and monitored

00:43:51.265 --> 00:43:53.390
and how often what you find
is what we've monitored

00:43:53.390 --> 00:43:55.760
is the thing that's
convenient versus the thing

00:43:55.760 --> 00:43:57.740
the customer cares about.

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:00.050
And then over a series of
outages and post-mortems,

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:01.310
you eventually migrate.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:04.280
So you can skip that whole
step, right, and go directly

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:08.270
to the great end result if you
take the time to write down

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:12.410
the SLOs that if you just barely
were meeting those things,

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:15.180
most of your customers
would be happy.

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:17.870
And then dog food them, right?

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:19.580
If you use your
product internally,

00:44:19.580 --> 00:44:23.660
and you should, try making
your internal instance

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:26.570
of your service operate where
it's just barely meeting

00:44:26.570 --> 00:44:29.180
those SLOs you just wrote and
see whether your internal users

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:29.680
are happy.

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:32.020
And if everyone's more
or less, yeah, you know,

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:36.110
seems slightly sluggish but
it's OK, you've got a good SLO.

00:44:36.110 --> 00:44:38.090
If everyone grabs their
torches and pitchforks

00:44:38.090 --> 00:44:42.110
and comes to your door, you
may want to revise, right?

00:44:42.110 --> 00:44:44.030
And secondly, we
talked about toil.

00:44:44.030 --> 00:44:46.340
Toil is-- it's like
technical debt,

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:49.820
right, except it's
much easier to measure.

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:52.400
And my recommendation is
look at your sources of toil

00:44:52.400 --> 00:44:54.800
and just pick the one that's
the highest ROI, right?

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:55.910
It's easy to fix.

00:44:55.910 --> 00:44:57.530
It occupies a lot
of people's time

00:44:57.530 --> 00:44:59.210
maybe because it
gets done a lot.

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:01.460
And start an engineering
project to actually fix that.

00:45:01.460 --> 00:45:03.350
Carve out folks' time
because it gives you

00:45:03.350 --> 00:45:04.850
this great experience
of now we've

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:08.420
spent n months of engineering,
and we've gotten rid

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:11.950
of n years per year of toil.

00:45:11.950 --> 00:45:13.560
OK, now I see how
the system works,

00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:16.143
and we can have a regular review
and sort of look for our toil

00:45:16.143 --> 00:45:18.410
and decide which ones
are worth eliminating.

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:20.030
And that get you
started on a path

00:45:20.030 --> 00:45:23.090
to avoid what we call
ops overload, which

00:45:23.090 --> 00:45:25.140
you don't want to be in.

00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:27.885
And then finally, everyone
has some production incident,

00:45:27.885 --> 00:45:30.260
right, whether it was an actual
full blown outage or just

00:45:30.260 --> 00:45:33.130
something that didn't go well.

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:34.490
Conduct a blameless post-mortem.

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:35.930
Try the approach
of saying, let me

00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:38.768
assume that although humans
will always make mistakes,

00:45:38.768 --> 00:45:41.060
I'm not going to focus on
that because I don't actually

00:45:41.060 --> 00:45:42.980
know how to fix that
in the real world.

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:44.743
But I can fix how
the system works.

00:45:44.743 --> 00:45:46.910
And conduct a post-mortem
where the conclusions are,

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:48.327
we should fix the
following things

00:45:48.327 --> 00:45:51.190
in the system to make
it less likely to fail,

00:45:51.190 --> 00:45:53.650
more likely to provide
good diagnostic information

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:56.500
when it does fail, make
the failure less severe,

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:58.780
make the failure
shorter, and see

00:45:58.780 --> 00:46:02.280
if you like that result because
my prediction is you will.

